      "writtenOn": "2019-06-06T17:41:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Intel Virtual MUX configuration code in the Kernel will be using this command at probe registration.\nUSB2 port of Type-C goes to PCH,\nUSB3 port of Type-C goes to SOC.\nAs USB max ports are only 8, I used 1byte data to fit both USB2 \u0026 USB3 port numbers.\nI will add this info or we can have two separate variables for usb2 \u0026 usb3. Which one would you prefer?",
      "parentUuid": "dcf752c0_b4d2e417",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4878,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 4888,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "192da7dd39288a3fd03d459f47593e1d2f510c88",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "64e05091_c3602b72",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4888,
      "author": {
        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-06-06T18:39:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "how about 2 different uint8_t variables: one for usb3 and one for usb2. bitX is port X. bitX is either set in usb2 or usb3 but not both so\n\nusb2 | usb3 \u003d\u003d (1 \u003c\u003c num_ports) - 1;\nusb2 \u0026 usb3 \u003d\u003d 0;\n\nin the case that a board provides this data,\n\nIf a board does not provide, then both variables will be 0\n\n(and we would need comments explain this encoding)",
      "parentUuid": "ed26c240_e7a5c1bf",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4878,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 4888,
        "endChar": 0
      },
